   Lee, Seonghui and O'Brien, Diana and Santoso, Lie Philip and Senk,
   Kaitlin and Stevenson, Randolph T.},
Title = {Gender inclusivity and perceptions of party ideology: Observational and
   experimental evidence},
Journal = {EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF POLITICAL RESEARCH},
Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 OCT 26},
Abstract = {Do citizens use gender-based heuristics to infer political parties'
   ideological positions? We posit that citizens view more `gender
   inclusive' parties as more left-leaning than comparable male-dominated
   organizations. Cues about gender inclusivity, moreover, come not only
   from women's presence in parties' legislative delegations but also from
   other visible signals of women's incorporation into parties - including
   as leaders and supporters - and through organizational rules promoting
   women's inclusion. We present analyses of election survey data from 21
   Western publics over time, which show that, controlling for the
   left-right tones of parties' manifestos, citizens perceive more
   gender-inclusive parties as more left-wing and that the cumulative
   impact of the various dimensions of gender inclusivity is substantial.
   We next report experimental evidence from four countries, which provides
   further support for gender-based heuristics. Our findings highlight the
   importance of not just the message but also the messenger in shaping
   citizens' assessments of parties' ideological perceptions.},
