Title = {A voter-led cordon sanitaire: coordination failure on the right
   and negative voting in Portugal's 2026 presidential elections},
Journal = {SOUTH EUROPEAN SOCIETY AND POLITICS},
Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 OCT 25},
Abstract = {Right-of-center candidates won close to two-thirds of the first-round
   vote in Portugal's 2026 presidential election, yet none reached the
   presidency. Right-wing fragmentation was a coordination failure that
   sent Andr \& eacute; Ventura (Chega; Enough) to the run-off, where Ant
   \& oacute;nio Jos \& eacute; Seguro (Socialist Party) beat him two to
   one. Right-wing voters coalesced against Ventura without party cues. For
   28.5 per cent of Seguro's voters this was negative voting: a ballot cast
   against Ventura, not for him. Among right-wing voters, attitudes and
   ideology distinguished those who crossed the left-right divide to vote
   for Seguro from those who chose Ventura, while partisanship did not.
   They drew a line their parties would not, because it rested on what they
   believed rather than on whom they followed.},
