Abstract = {Right-wing populist (RWP) governments are widely regarded as major
   challengers of the liberal international order (LIO), yet existing
   scholarship has not systematically established why they contest some
   international organizations more strongly than others. Is such
   contestation driven by populist opposition to supranational authority,
   or by substantive conflicts between right-wing ideology and particular
   international organizations' agendas? We distinguish these mechanisms
   and derive expectations across the domains of human rights and the rule
   of law, trade, and security. To test them, we draw on an original
   cross-national expert survey measuring RWP governments' approval of
   individual international organizations in 13 countries. The results show
   that an organization's agenda matters more consistently than its
   authority. RWP governments are least supportive of organizations
   promoting human rights and the rule of law, display mixed attitudes
   toward trade-related bodies, and are more approving of security
   organizations. In the security domain, greater international
   organization authority is associated with higher rather than lower
   approval. These findings challenge explanations that attribute RWP
   contestation primarily to populist anti-elitism or generalized
   resistance to supranational authority. Instead, such contestation is
   selective and reflects a deeper conflict between right-wing ideological
   commitments and the egalitarian norms advanced by parts of the LIO.},
