Title = {From identity to caring: A 44-country examination of the role of global
   social identifications in concern for global issues},
Journal = {POLITICAL PSYCHOLOGY},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {47},
Number = {6},
Month = {OCT 2},
Abstract = {Previous research suggests that people who identify with global
   communities tend to show greater concern for global challenges. Using
   student samples from 44 countries across different cultural clusters (N
   = 13,055), we examined whether (1) three forms of dispositional global
   social identification (GSI, with all humanity, citizens of the world,
   and people all over the world) are related to concern for global issues,
   (2) situational activation of these identifications increases such
   concern, (3) country-level traditional and survival values are
   negatively associated with global concern, and (4) these values moderate
   the GSI-concern link. Both dispositional and situational GSIs positively
   predicted global concern. Situational activation showed inconsistent
   effects, whereas dispositional GSIs demonstrated moderate and consistent
   associations with global concern across the full sample and most
   countries. Overall, dispositional forms of GSI appeared more influential
   than situational ones. Contrary to expectations, traditional values (and
   survival values-marginally) were positively related to global concern
   and showed no evidence of moderating GSI-concern associations.
   Additional analyses showed the indices of GDP per capita, Human
   Development, Gini, and poverty as robust predictors of global concern.
   We discuss how these findings refine claims about the universality of
   global identification outcomes and highlight the importance of
   socio-cultural context in shaping such concern.},
