Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {One of the first things travelers to the Indian Himalaya remark upon are
   the curious, often rhyming and comedic, road signs of the Border Roads
   Organisation (BRO) dotting the strategic roads of the region. In this
   article, I ask what role road signs play in physically narrating,
   constructing and altering the imagination of the contested high-altitude
   region of Ladakh. I provide a combined discursive and visual analysis of
   military-origin road signs in Ladakh, drawing on a series of fieldwork
   trips across 2019-2025. I make two core contributions. First, I add to
   the literature on the political geography of road signs, by
   demonstrating their expansive role in re-narrating an entire region.
   Second, I contribute to our understanding of Ladakh's geopolitics and
   its place in the Indian state. Specifically, I argue that road signs are
   an important means of marking contested territory for the Indian Army,
   and construct specific ideas of nationalism, sacrifice, masculinity, and
   safety. The crucial narrative of these road signs is India's military
   prowess and engineering skill, making the region safe for both the
   nation and for tourism. These ideas are blended with gender,
   particularly hypermasculinity, and the need to dominate nature for the
   state.},
